# Pin line endings so a file's bytes in the repository do not depend on who
# checked it out.
#
# This was not cosmetic. With no file here, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows
# converted LF to CRLF on checkout while the repository stored LF, so a file
# that no one edited showed as modified with an empty diff:
#
#     git status  ->  M crates/omnidesk-core/src/relay.rs
#     git diff    ->  (nothing)
#
# Which means "is the tree clean?" had an answer that was wrong in a way that
# looks like noise. Any gate reading `git status --porcelain` to decide
# whether a run started from a known state would read "changed" and could not
# tell it apart from a real edit.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; the two `* text=...` lines pin
# the two file kinds this repository actually has. Rust and Python are LF in
# the repository and CRLF in a Windows checkout, which is what `autocrlf`
# would have done anyway -- the difference is that it is now a stated property
# of the repository rather than a property of each developer's config.
#
# The `*.sh` and `*.bat` split is the reason this is worth writing down: the
# push watcher and any shell tooling must stay LF, because a CRLF shebang
# makes `/bin/sh` fail on a line ending rather than on a real error.

* text=auto

*.rs      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf

# Windows-native launchers must keep CRLF; cmd.exe does not accept LF-only
# batch files reliably.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf

# Not text. `text=auto` leaves these alone, and naming them stops a future
# `* text=auto` from being read as applying to them.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.ico     binary
*.exe     binary
*.pdf     binary
